A||||8||deceler8||Anyone with a R134 retrofit...||||5||
Z||000000||deceler8||03-01-2000||10:42 AM||||How's it holding up and what were the steps you took...<P>Just wondering if the stock Fiero compressor is holding up and what (if anything) you replaced prior to doing the conversion.<P>I did one of these on a previous car (replaced compressor, dryer, flushed and used barrier hoses) and didn't notice a difference in the way it cooled (seemed to work as well as R12).<P>Anyone...<P>Am looking to do the conversion on an '86 2m4 with non-working air (assuming lost charge).||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||03-01-2000||02:48 PM||||If it lost the charge it must have leaked out somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||03-01-2000||03:19 PM||||I don't know if it's all gone or if there's not enough to engage the compressor.<P>If I had a can of R12, I'd try it to see.<P>I suspect that this car hadn't been driven much prior to my buying it so it could have slowly leaked out. When I tackle this I will certainly replace the O rings and dryer.<P>Just wondering how well some of the retrofits are doing with time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||03-01-2000||04:12 PM||||I converted my 84 to R-134 about two years ago.  My old compressor had failed, so I put a new one on.  I also replaced the dryer and orifice tube.  I did not replace any of the lines.  I used a conversion kit made by Everco from a local auto parts store.  It included the service fitting adapters and oil for the system.  The instructions said to remove as much of the old oil as practical, but the oil in the kit was compatible with R-12 oil, so don't worry about getting it all.  I can't tell any difference in the cooling with the 134-I can still form icicles at will!  After 2 years I've had absolutely no problems with the conversion.  Since then I have done conversions on an 84 Audi 5000, a 72 Chevelle, and a few other GM products all with excellent results.  The Fiero cost about $100 for the kit, dryer and orifice tube and was money well spent.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Phil||03-01-2000||04:15 PM||||I did one on my in-progress kit car I had to replace my compressor (cracked) dryer,oriface tube and O rings. I dumped the mineral oil out of the new comp. poured in the ester oil put everything back together evacuated the system and charged it up. I start the car up about once a month and check everything out. So far the air has been fine for about a year. One suggestion- buy a pressure gage ($10) so you don't have to guess on the charge weight.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||03-01-2000||05:00 PM||||where did you get a $10 pressure guage?  The parts stores here want $100 for a set of R-134 guages.||||reg||5
Z||000006||Phil||03-01-2000||05:51 PM||||It's just a single pressure gage with adapters to fit the high or low side fittings . I got it at Pep Boys -Interdynamics made in China -it's just a 500psi pressure gage and comes with a temp/pressure chart||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||03-02-2000||11:06 AM||||No PEP Boys here.  I'll keep it in mind thou.<P>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000008||Carrolles||03-02-2000||12:48 PM||||IWhen I installed my new clutch and new engine _v-6) in my 85se I replaced a locked up A/C compressor with a new one.  I also replaced the drier and orifice tube.  While the engine was out I pulled the drier out and flushed the system with flush oil.  I simply poured some in the line that connects to the compressor and blew it through with my air compressor.  I then replaced all of the o-rings.  This didn't take too long although I did remove the spare tire trunk liner to get easier access to the line that runs along the bottom of it.  I bought an R-134 conversion kit that had an 8 oz. bottle of oil.  I poured the entire 8 oz. of oil into the compressor and turned it over by hand about 5 or 6 revolutions.<BR>I isntalled the compressor, hooked up the lines, installed the new drier and orifice tube, installed the connector port fittings, pulled vacuum and charged it up.  So far it is working great with no noticible difference from the R-12.||||reg||7
